Now I have the full picture of domain/work/thread.ts. My changes there:

1. `TurnReaders` gains a `claim` reader: `(body: string) => { ask: number; run: number } | undefined` (from TOOK_TAG). And the `ending` reader — the fold needs to know what an ending ANSWERS. The `ending` reader returns just the event kind. I need `answered` too. Options: extend TurnReaders with `answeredAsk: (body) => number | undefined` (from ENDED_TAG.answers). And `agentOf`? The dispatch check needs the answering ending's AGENT (AGENT_TAG). Hmm — AGENT_TAG is machinery-level; the domain readers are passed in by the adapter, so I can add `answeredAgent: (body) => string` — hmm, that's growing. Alternative: keep TurnReaders' events and ADD the identity fold as a SEPARATE structure in domain/work/run-report.ts (where the watch's readers live — `EndingWalkReaders`, `WatchComment`). Hmm.

Wait — the domain already has TWO reader interfaces: `TurnReaders` (thread.ts) and `EndingWalkReaders`/`WatchComment` (run-report.ts). The claim/ending identity fold needs: for each comment: {id?, body, isHuman} → took {ask, run}, ended {verb, answers, run, agent?}. The AGENT comes from AGENT_TAG — an adapter-level tag. The dispatch check's "own verdict" branch needs the agent name — but wait: the check receives `d.agent` (the dispatched agent) — the comparison "answering ending's agent == dispatched agent" can be done in the ADAPTER (dispatch.ts) by reading AGENT_TAG there, OR the domain function takes a `answeredBy(body): string` reader.

Cleanest per this repo's architecture: the FOLD lives in domain/work/run-report.ts with its own readers interface (already has EndingWalkReaders — let me look at run-report.ts's reader interface and WatchComment). The watch already reads comments via `readRunWatchThread` (adapters/github/thread.ts) → `RunWatchThread`. The dispatch check can reuse the SAME thread read + the SAME domain fold. 

Let me look at run-report.ts's types (WatchComment, EndingWalkReaders) and readRunWatchThread's shape. Then design:

**domain/work/run-report.ts** (the turn-identity fold):
```ts
export interface IdentityComment { id: number; body: string; isHuman: boolean; quoted?: boolean }
export interface IdentityReaders {
  claim: (body: string) => { ask: number; run: number } | undefined;
  ending: (body: string) => { verb: string; answers: number; run: number } | undefined;
  answeredBy: (body: string) => string;   // AGENT_TAG, "" when none
  requestedAgent: (body: string) => string;
  isDispatchMarker: (body: string) => boolean;
}
```
Hmm — that's a lot of readers. Alternatively the domain defines the fold over ALREADY-PARSED structures: the adapter parses each comment into a small record and the domain folds records. That's cleaner for testing:

```ts
/** One comment as the turn-identity fold reads it. */
export interface TurnMark {
  id: number;
  /** The claim a took tag carries, when the comment is one. */
  claim?: { ask: number; run: number };
  /** The ending an ended tag carries, when the comment is one. */
  ending?: { verb: string; answers: number; run: number };
  /** The agent the comment's result tag names, when it does. */
  agent?: string;
  /** The agent a command or marker asks for, when it does. */
  requested?: string;
  /** Whether the machinery wrote it (a dispatch marker). */
  marker?: boolean;
  isHuman: boolean;
  quoted?: boolean;
}
export function foldTurnMarks(marks: readonly TurnMark[]): TurnFold  // { asks: AskState[]; claims; endings; ... }
```

Hmm — wait. But `whoseTurn`/`shapedThread` also need the claim fold, and they work on ThreadEntry + TurnReaders (bodies). Mixing two models (bodies+readers here, parsed marks there) risks drift. 

Let me reconsider: the MINIMAL change to thread.ts that implements the design's guard rule:

```ts
export interface TurnReaders {
  ending: (body) => "returned" | "handed-off" | "waiting" | "";
  claim: (body) => { ask: number; run: number } | undefined;   // NEW
  answeredAsk: (body) => number | undefined;                    // NEW — the ask an ending answers (0 = none)
  requestedAgent: (body) => string;
  isDispatchMarker: (body) => boolean;
