```

- `shapedThread`: the walk tracks `openClaimAsk: number | undefined`:
  ```ts
  for (const entry of comments) {
    if (entry.isHuman && !entry.quoted && openClaimAsk !== undefined) continue;  // ball with an agent
    kept.push(entry);
    // update the claim state from THIS entry (after the keep/drop decision — the dropped comment doesn't move the turn)
    const claim = readers.claim(entry.body);
    if (claim) openClaimAsk = claim.ask;
    else if (openClaimAsk !== undefined) {
      const answered = readers.answeredAsk(entry.body);
      if (answered !== undefined && answered === openClaimAsk) openClaimAsk = undefined;
    }
    const event = eventOf(entry, readers);
    if (event !== undefined) events.push(event);
  }
  ```
  Wait — ordering: a claim comment IS a machinery comment (kept); the ending comment is machinery too (kept). Only human comments get dropped. The claim/ending state updates should happen for kept entries only? If a human comment carried a took tag in prose... readers.claim reads the TAG — a person could write `<!-- atomaton:took=... -->`? Person comments with machinery tags: the tag is read as prose? Hmm — tags are read from bodies regardless of author today (a person CAN write a tag...). Edge case; the current code reads endings from any non-quoted body. Keep consistent: read claim/answered from non-quoted entries (machinery or human) — consistent with eventOf which reads endings from any non-quoted entry.

- `whoseTurn(events)`: callers pass events — events no longer know claims! Callers: let me grep. If whoseTurn is only called by shapedThread and tests (plus maybe runInFlight/commentWouldBeRemoved via shaped events), I can REPLACE whoseTurn(events) with a claim-aware variant. Let me grep whoseTurn usage.

- The events array itself: unchanged (asked/handed-off/waiting/returned from endings + asked from commands/markers) — requestOutstanding keeps working on it ✓ (unchanged behavior for the ordering check).

- For the DISPATCH CHECK: a domain function over entries+readers:
  ```ts
